# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line-ending policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Windows is the primary platform here, and `core.autocrlf=true` is a very
# common global setting. Without explicit rules git rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout, which silently breaks anything that must keep LF.
#
# The concrete failure this prevents: start-bridge.sh is stored with LF, but a
# checkout on Windows turned it into CRLF, so its first line became
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r". bash then reports the interpreter path as not found
# and the script cannot run at all. Cloning the repository was enough to break
# it — no edit required.

# Default: normalise to LF in the repository and on checkout, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF. A CR in the shebang breaks execution.
*.sh text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files git must not touch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Windows batch files are generated in the console codepage (GBK) with CRLF.
# Marking them binary keeps their bytes exactly as generated: a GBK file
# normalised as text could have its Chinese text corrupted by encoding
# conversion during checkout.
*.cmd -text
*.cmd.utf8 -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows-specific text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# VBScript runs under wscript.exe, where CRLF is conventional.
*.vbs text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary assets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.crx binary
*.pem binary
